<https://w3id.org/project-chemie-digital/graph/scenes/level5-depth> {
  ex:dep-functions a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:cumulative-distribution-function ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-functions-h, ex:dep-functions-c ; cd:narration "Use one pipette uncertainty model for three practical questions. We assume the volume is equally plausible from 9.98 to 10.02 mL. These bounds and equal plausibility are model assumptions, not guaranteed coverage from a manufacturer's tolerance. A PDF describes local density: integrate over an acceptable volume range to obtain its probability. A CDF accumulates this density up to a chosen volume threshold. An inverse CDF reverses the question: supply a cumulative probability and return the corresponding volume. First learn these operations on a normalized uniform input from zero to one. Then map that input back to physical volumes and compare both plots for the same event. This assumed input model will recur in Monte Carlo propagation."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-functions-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "One pipette, three analytical questions"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-functions-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-functions-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-functions-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-functions-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-paired a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:cumulative-distribution-function ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-functions ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-paired-h, ex:dep-paired-v, ex:dep-paired-f ; cd:narration "The CDF is the accumulated PDF area, not a second unrelated curve. Left, the uniform PDF gives area 0.50 between normalized values 0.25 and 0.75. Right, subtract cumulative heights 0.75 and 0.25 to obtain exactly the same probability. The CDF's slope is density within the support; CDF height is probability. Map the normalized input to volume: 9.98 plus 0.04 times X in mL. The same interval becomes 9.99 to 10.01 mL. Its density is 25 per mL, so width 0.02 mL times density 25 per mL gives probability 0.50. Up to 10.01 mL the probability is 0.75. Reverse that CDF and the 75th percentile is 10.01 mL. Physical values and probabilities no longer share the same numerical coordinates, making their different roles explicit. These are conditional model statements, not experimental calibration evidence."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-paired-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "PDF area and CDF difference answer the same question"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-paired-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-paired-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-paired-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-pair-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-paired-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-pipette-formula ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-sigma a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:normal-measurement-model ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-normal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-sigma-h, ex:dep-sigma-v, ex:dep-sigma-f ; cd:narration "Return to individual nitrate control results with specified normal mean 10.00 and SD 0.30 mg/L. One SD on each side gives 9.70 to 10.30 mg/L and probability 68.2689 percent. Two give 9.40 to 10.60 and 95.4500 percent. Three give 9.10 to 10.90 and 99.7300 percent. These are nested model ranges, not absolute limits or mean confidence intervals. Phi, introduced here as the standard-normal cumulative distribution function, lets us evaluate any such standardized range. By symmetry the upper cumulative probability minus the lower is twice Phi at k minus one. For an exactly central 95 percent normal range use approximately 1.95996 SDs, not two. Reporting these bands requires the normal model and a meaningful population of individual results; estimated parameters add uncertainty, and a systematic bias can move all results without changing this spread."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-sigma-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Normal reporting ranges: one, two and three SDs"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-sigma-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-sigma-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-sigma-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-normal-bands-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-sigma-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-normal-bands ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-log-story a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:dep-log-size ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-lognormal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-log-story-h, ex:dep-log-story-c ; cd:narration "A hypothetical aerosol microscopy study measures individual particle diameters. The analytical questions are the fraction exceeding 5 micrometres and a useful central size range. We model number-weighted diameters: each particle counts once. A mass-weighted distribution would answer a different question. Relative growth effects can multiply diameters and motivate a lognormal candidate, but this mechanism must be checked rather than inferred from a right-skewed histogram. Specify what particles enter the microscope, the diameter definition and detection window. Size-dependent detection, mixtures of particle sources, agglomeration and truncated sampling can invalidate a single lognormal law. All parameters here are supplied teaching assumptions, not fitted measurements or a claim about an actual aerosol. The process schematic contrasts this relative growth with additive analytical error; the size-model calculation applies that rationale to the microscopy question."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-story-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Lognormal usecase: which particle sizes should we report"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-story-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-story-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-story-c a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-cards ; cd:communicativeRole cd:DefinitionListRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:hasDefinitionListEntry" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-log-process a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:dep-log-size ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-lognormal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-log-process-h, ex:dep-log-process-v ; cd:narration "An additive model describes absolute increments; this candidate model describes relative changes. Each positive factor multiplies the current diameter. Taking the natural log of diameter divided by a fixed reference length converts that product into a sum of log factors. If many suitably small, non-dominant log contributions with appropriate dependence and finite variance produce an approximately normal sum, the original diameter is approximately lognormal. Exactly normal log size defines an exact lognormal model. Three illustrated stages are a schematic, not a proof. Random additive diameter increments, strong correlated growth, multiple sources and agglomeration may need another model. The reference length prevents taking a logarithm of a dimensional quantity. Next state the parameters, inspect the size curve and use it to answer the microscopy question."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-process-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "From relative growth effects to a lognormal model"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-process-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-process-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-process-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-process-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-log-model a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:dep-log-size ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-lognormal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-log-model-h, ex:dep-log-model-v, ex:dep-log-model-f ; cd:narration "The product identity explains why log size is useful; the normal law on that log scale is a separate assumption. Diameter D is positive and measured in micrometres. Choose reference length one micrometre, log mean ln(2) and log SD 0.75. Thus the central diameter is two micrometres and the geometric SD is exp(0.75), about 2.117. These parameters do not describe an arithmetic diameter SD. The plotted density has units per micrometre and is evaluated from this specified lognormal law, not fitted observations. Straight segments connect drawing coordinates; areas from the finite polyline are not the numerical probability engine. Software evaluates the model CDF and quantiles using the normal log scale. We now use that bridge for a threshold, multiplicative sigma ranges and an exactly central 95 percent report."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-model-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Specify the size model on the log scale"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-model-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-model-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-model-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-size-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-model-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-law ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-log-threshold a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:dep-log-size ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-lognormal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-log-threshold-h, ex:dep-log-threshold-v, ex:dep-log-threshold-f ; cd:narration "Answer the microscopy question: what fraction of individual diameters exceed five micrometres? Divide that threshold by the one-micrometre reference, take natural log, subtract ln(2) and divide by 0.75. The standardized log threshold is about 1.22172. The normal upper tail is about 0.11091, so 11.09 percent of the modeled number-weighted population exceeds five micrometres. The shaded drawing stops at 20 micrometres, but the probability calculation includes the complete infinite tail. This is not a confidence statement about the population mean or evidence that a real sampled aerosol has this fraction. Extrapolation beyond the observed detection window requires particular caution. A report should state both the size definition and the assumed model."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-threshold-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Apply the model: fraction of diameters above 5 µm"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-threshold-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-threshold-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-threshold-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-tail-chart ; cd:communicativeRole cd:ChartRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:body" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-threshold-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-tail ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-log-ranges a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:dep-log-size ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-lognormal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-log-ranges-h, ex:dep-log-ranges-v, ex:dep-log-ranges-f ; cd:narration "There is a direct analogue of normal one-, two- and three-SD bands, but it acts on the log scale. Start at log mean and move k log SDs in both directions, then exponentiate. On the original scale this divides and multiplies the central diameter by the geometric SD raised to k. Here the centre is two micrometres and geometric SD is about 2.117. One log SD gives 0.9447 to 4.2340 micrometres; two give 0.4463 to 8.9634; three give 0.2108 to 18.9755. Coverage remains 68.27, 95.45 and 99.73 percent because the underlying log size is normal. Bounds are symmetric in log coordinates, not in micrometres. Do not write centre plus or minus geometric SD: it is a factor without length units. These ranges concern individual sizes under a specified model, not confidence intervals for a mean or median."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-ranges-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Lognormal sigma ranges multiply and divide"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-ranges-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-ranges-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-ranges-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-bands-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-ranges-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 4 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-bands ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-log-reporting a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:dep-log-size ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-lognormal ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-log-reporting-h, ex:dep-log-reporting-f ; cd:narration "Finish the analytical report by choosing coverage explicitly instead of automatically calling two log SDs 95 percent. Invert the normal CDF at probabilities 0.025 and 0.975, multiply those log coordinates by 0.75, add ln(2), exponentiate and restore the one-micrometre reference. This yields a central 95 percent model size range from 0.4599 to 8.6982 micrometres. A suitable statement is: under the specified number-weighted lognormal diameter model, the central 95 percent of individual particle diameters lies in this range. The threshold result of 11.09 percent above five micrometres answers a separate question about the same model. Neither result quantifies parameter-estimation uncertainty. For fitted data, report estimation method, sample/detection scope, model checks and appropriate uncertainty for estimated quantities separately. A 95 percent size range is not a confidence interval for a population mean."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-reporting-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Report a central 95% size range, not a mean interval"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-reporting-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-reporting-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-log-reporting-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 3 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-log-report ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-origins a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-origins-h, ex:dep-origins-v ; cd:narration "The next distributions do not replace the shape of the raw concentration measurements. They describe statistics calculated from repeated samples. Squared independent standard-normal coordinates give chi-square; estimating a common mean removes one independent coordinate and leaves n minus one residual degrees of freedom. A normal numerator divided by an independent uncertain scale gives Student-t. A ratio of two independent variance scales gives F. These constructions explain why the distributions are useful for repeatability variance, uncertainty in a mean when SD is estimated, and comparisons of method precision. Independence and normality supply the exact sampling references. Non-normal observations, paired series or drift may need different methods, not merely a different degrees-of-freedom setting. We now build each reference briefly, then apply it to its analytical question."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-origins-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "From analytical results to distributions of statistics"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-origins-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-origins-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-origins-v a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-origins-media ; cd:communicativeRole cd:MediaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:uri" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-chi-construction a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:al-chi-square ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-chi ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-chi-h, ex:dep-chi-f ; cd:narration "Chi-square arises by adding squared independent standard-normal coordinates. Negative and positive deviations both contribute positively. Four chosen coordinates 1, minus 1, zero and 2 give a realized sum six. That arithmetic illustrates a construction; it is not a measurement series or evidence of its distribution. In the repeatability study, subtracting the fitted sample mean imposes a constraint: the centered raw residuals sum to zero and are not independent. An orthogonal change of coordinates leaves n minus one independent normal residual coordinates. Their squared sum is the sample variance multiplied by n minus one and divided by population variance. This is why five independent normal results give four degrees of freedom. Next inspect the sampling reference and invert its quantiles to put bounds on the unknown repeatability variance. The interval concerns variance, not individual concentration values."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-chi-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Why chi-square: independent squared deviations add"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-chi-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-chi-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-chi-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-chi-build ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-t-construction a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-student-t ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-t ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-t-h, ex:dep-t-f ; cd:narration "If population SD were known, the normal sample mean could be standardized by that known scale. In practice the same small analytical series estimates SD, so the denominator fluctuates. Student-t combines a standard-normal numerator with the square root of an independent chi-square divided by its degrees of freedom. A small realized denominator makes the ratio more extreme: here a normal coordinate of one and chi-square value one with four degrees of freedom give a t value of two. Under independent normal sampling, the sample mean and variance are independent, supplying exactly the needed construction. The resulting heavier tails account for estimating SD and make small-sample mean intervals wider than a known-SD normal calculation. As degrees of freedom grow the estimated scale stabilizes and t approaches standard normal. The next formula connects this construction to five laboratory results and then reports the mean interval."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-t-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Why Student-t: the estimated denominator fluctuates"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-t-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-t-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-t-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-t-build ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .

  ex:dep-f-construction a cd:SceneDefinition ; cd:focusConcept ex:l5-f-distribution ; dct:subject ex:l5-topic-f ; cd:hasSceneItem ex:dep-f-h, ex:dep-f-f ; cd:narration "Each method's estimated variance fluctuates. Under independent normal sampling, each variance divided by its population variance is chi-square divided by its own residual degrees of freedom. Taking the ratio of two independent scaled chi-squares gives F, with ordered numerator and denominator degrees of freedom. The illustrative scaled values two and one half give a ratio four, matching the later observed variance ratio. This is not a new dataset. With six results in each series there are five degrees of freedom on each side. Under equal population variances those unknown variances cancel and the raw sample-variance ratio has the F reference. Under unequal population variances that raw ratio is scaled by the unknown population ratio. Prespecifying B over A and the direction of the alternative matters; reversing after seeing which variance is larger changes the test. The next slides apply this reference to method precision, not method bias or accuracy."@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-f-title a cd:Interpretation ; skos:prefLabel "Why F: compare two independent variance estimates"@en ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-f-h a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 1 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-f-title ; cd:communicativeRole cd:HeadingRole ; cd:selectionPath "skos:prefLabel@en" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
  ex:dep-f-f a cd:SceneItem ; cd:position 2 ; cd:selectsResource ex:dep-f-build ; cd:communicativeRole cd:FormulaRole ; cd:selectionPath "cd:latex" ; cd:language "en" ; cd:authoredResource true .
}
